.\" pogemini(1): the manual page for pogemini, a Gemini server.
.\" mdoc, so it reads the same with mandoc (the BSDs) and groff (Linux).
.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt POGEMINI 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pogemini
.Nd a server for the Gemini protocol
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl config Ar file
.Op Fl listen Ar host
.Op Fl port Ar port
.Op Fl deadline Ar ms
.Op Fl connections Ar n
.Op Fl user Ar name
.Op Fl certdir Ar dir
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Op Fl host Ar name ...
.Op Fl root Ar dir Op Fl listings
.Op Fl cert Ar file Fl key Ar file
.Op Fl require Ar path ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
serves capsules, sites for the Gemini protocol, over TLS: for each, the
files under its root directory.
It serves every connection in a task of its own, in one thread, so a
slow or stalled client holds up no other, and gives each connection a
deadline for the whole exchange.
.Pp
The capsules come from a configuration file,
.Fl config ,
and one more may be given on the command line: its name is the first
.Fl host ,
any others its aliases, with
.Fl root ,
.Fl cert ,
.Fl key
and
.Fl require .
It comes before those in the file.
Without
.Fl host ,
it is a capsule for any host name, and needs
.Fl cert
and
.Fl key .
The other options override the file's settings of the same name,
described under
.Sx CONFIGURATION .
At least one capsule must be given.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-connections n"
.It Fl config Ar file
Read the configuration from
.Ar file .
.It Fl listen Ar host
Listen on the address
.Ar host
only, rather than on every local address, IPv6 and IPv4.
.It Fl port Ar port
Listen on
.Ar port ;
the default is 1965, Gemini's.
With 0, the kernel chooses one.
.It Fl deadline Ar ms
Give each connection
.Ar ms
milliseconds, from the TLS handshake to its end; 10000 by default, at
most 3600000.
.It Fl connections Ar n
Serve at most
.Ar n
connections at once, 256 by default, at most 100000; the rest wait in
the kernel's listen queue until one ends.
.It Fl user Ar name
Started as root, serve as the user
.Ar name ;
see
.Sx SECURITY .
.It Fl certdir Ar dir
Make and keep self-signed certificates in
.Ar dir ;
see
.Sx CERTIFICATES .
.It Fl n Ar count
Serve
.Ar count
connections, wait for them to end, and exit 0; for testing.
.It Fl host Ar name
The command line's capsule's name, or, after the first, an alias.
.It Fl root Ar dir
Its root: the files it serves.
.It Fl listings
List its directories that have no
.Pa index.gmi ,
as
.Ic listings yes
does; it needs
.Fl root .
.It Fl cert Ar file Fl key Ar file
Its certificate and private key, in PEM.
.It Fl require Ar path
A path in it that needs a client certificate, any; see
.Sx CLIENT CERTIFICATES .
.El
.Pp
Its first line on standard output is
.Dq port Ar n ,
the port it listens on, so that a program that started it on port 0
learns which the kernel chose.
Then it logs a line per connection, beginning with the time, UTC:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<time> <client address> <status> <request> <client certificate | ->
<time> <client address> failed: <why>
.Ed
.Pp
The request is written with its control characters as
.Li %XX ,
so a line is always one line, and a client certificate as its SHA-256
hash,
.Li SHA256:
and 64 hexadecimal digits.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The file has a line per setting: a keyword, then its value, the rest
of the line with its spaces trimmed, so a file name may contain spaces.
A name or a number is one word.
.Ql #
begins a comment, and blank lines are ignored.
The global settings come first:
.Bl -tag -width "connections n"
.It Ic listen Ar host
As
.Fl listen .
.It Ic port Ar port
As
.Fl port .
.It Ic deadline Ar ms
As
.Fl deadline .
.It Ic connections Ar n
As
.Fl connections .
.It Ic user Ar name
As
.Fl user .
.It Ic certdir Ar dir
As
.Fl certdir .
.El
.Pp
Then each capsule, everything from its
.Ic capsule
line to the next:
.Bl -tag -width "identity hash"
.It Ic capsule Ar name
A capsule, for the host
.Ar name ,
a DNS name or an IP address, written plainly
.Pq Li ::1 , not Li [::1] ,
in ASCII; an internationalised name is written in its
.Li xn--
form.
.It Ic alias Ar name
Another name for it.
.It Ic root Ar dir
Its root.
Without one, it answers
.Li /
with a page naming it, and anything else with 51.
.It Ic cert Ar file
Its certificate, in PEM.
.It Ic key Ar file
Its private key, in PEM.
.It Ic listings yes | no
Whether a directory without
.Pa index.gmi
is listed; see
.Sx FILES SERVED .
No unless given.
.It Ic require Ar path
A path that needs a client certificate.
.It Ic identity Ar hash
After a
.Ic require :
a client certificate it accepts, by its SHA-256 hash, as the log shows
it.
.El
.Pp
The first capsule is the one shown to a client that names no host in
TLS (SNI), and the one whose certificate it sees.
A request is answered by the capsule whose name or alias its host is,
or else by the one for any host.
A request for a host no capsule has, or for another port than the one
listened on, is refused with 53:
.Nm
is no proxy.
.Pp
.Pa contrib/pogemini.conf ,
installed in the documentation directory, is an example.
.Sh FILES SERVED
A request's path names a file under the capsule's root:
.Li /a/b.gmi
is
.Pa <root>/a/b.gmi .
A regular file is served with status 20 and the MIME type its
extension gives,
.Li text/gemini
for
.Pa .gmi
and
.Pa .gemini ,
and
.Li application/octet-stream
for one unknown; text is taken to be UTF-8.
A directory asked for with a final
.Ql /
is served its
.Pa index.gmi ;
asked for without, it is redirected (31) to the same path with the
.Ql /
added.
A directory without
.Pa index.gmi
is answered 51, unless its capsule has
.Ic listings yes
.Pq or Fl listings :
then it is listed, as a
.Li text/gemini
page with a link to its parent and to each file and directory in it
that would be served, directories first, then files, each by the bytes
of their names.
A listing never shows a hidden name, anything a request for it would
be refused, nor anything in a
.Ic require
the client may not have; at most 10000 names are listed, with a line
saying there are more.
Listings are off unless asked for, since they show files the capsule
may never have linked to.
.Pp
Everything else is answered 51, not found: a name that isn't there,
anything not a regular file, anything the server can't read, a name
beginning with
.Ql \&.
.Pq Pa .git ,
and anything whose real path, once symbolic links are resolved, lies
outside the root or passes through such a name.
A symbolic link from one file under the root to another is followed.
A failure of the server's own, such as too many files open, is 40.
.Pp
A request that isn't a URL, or that the specification forbids (over
1024 bytes, with user information or a fragment, or a path that climbs
above the root), is refused with 59; one for another scheme than
.Li gemini ,
with 53.
.Sh CLIENT CERTIFICATES
A request for a path within a
.Ic require
.Pq or Fl require
needs a client certificate.
A require covers its path and everything below it, segment by segment:
.Li /private
covers
.Li /private/a.gmi ,
not
.Li /privateer ,
and
.Li /
covers the whole capsule.
Without a certificate the request is answered 60, with one outside its
dates 62, and, if
.Ic identity
lines follow the require, with one not among them 61.
Where requires nest, each applies.
A symbolic link can't lead into a require from outside it.
.Pp
Client certificates are self-signed, as Gemini's are, and are
checked against no authority.
.Sh CERTIFICATES
A capsule given neither
.Ic cert
nor
.Ic key
gets a self-signed certificate, ECDSA P-256, for its name and aliases,
valid ten years.
It is made the first time, and kept in the certificate directory as
.Pa <name>.crt
and
.Pa <name>.key ,
the key readable by its owner only; later runs use it as it is.
A certificate directory made then is readable by its owner only too;
one that was there keeps its mode.
A key put there alone, as
.Pa <name>.key ,
is kept and given its certificate.
Clients pin the certificate they first see from a capsule, so keep the
directory: a new certificate would look to them like an attack.
A capsule for any host must be given its certificate and key.
.Sh SECURITY
Started as root,
.Nm
becomes
.Ic user ,
with that user's groups, once it has read its keys and listens, so
keys only root can read and ports below 1024 can be used, and files are
served as that user.
Started as root without one it refuses to run, since a server reading
files as root is one mistake from serving any of them;
.Fl user Ar root
keeps root for whoever really wants it.
Not started as root,
.Ic user
may name only the user it runs as.
Certificates are made before the change, so as root, in root's
certificate directory unless
.Ic certdir
names another.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width "SIGTERM, SIGINT"
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Stop: close the listeners, so new clients are refused at once, wait for
the connections in progress, which their deadline bounds, and exit 0,
logging
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<time> stopping on signal <n>, connections in progress: <k>
<time> stopped
.Ed
.Pp
A second signal of the same kind ends it at once.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "XDG_DATA_HOME"
.It Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
The default certificate directory is
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/pogemini/certs .
.It Ev HOME
Without
.Ev XDG_DATA_HOME ,
it is
.Pa $HOME/.local/share/pogemini/certs .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 after a stop by a signal, or after
.Fl n Ar count
connections; 1 for a mistake in the configuration, or a failure to
start (a certificate that can't be read or made, an address that can't
be listened on, a user it can't become); 2 for a mistake in the
options.
A mistake is reported on standard error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Serve a directory, with a certificate made the first time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pogemini -host example.org -root /srv/gemini/example.org
.Ed
.Pp
Serve the capsules in a file, started as root and serving as
.Li gemini :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pogemini -config /etc/pogemini.conf -user gemini
.Ed
.Pp
A configuration with two capsules, one with its own certificate and a
part for a single client certificate:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
port 1965
user gemini
certdir /var/lib/pogemini/certs

capsule example.org
  alias www.example.org
  root /srv/gemini/example.org
  cert /etc/pogemini/example.org.crt
  key /etc/pogemini/example.org.key
  require /private
    identity SHA256:3f0c...

capsule example.net
  root /srv/gemini/example.net
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tls_init 3
.Rs
.%T Gemini network protocol specification
.%U gemini://geminiprotocol.net/docs/protocol-specification.gmi
.Re
.Sh AUTHORS
.An T. Kurt Bond ,
with the aid of AI.
